From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 16:05:10 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] nsiproxy: iOS: reuse one interface snapshot for a second

The Witcher 3 profile spends 27 percent of its main thread in interface
queries. Its sampled call site is witcher3.exe+2546470, a call to
InternetGetConnectedState in the services::Galaxy vtable. Wine answers
that API with GetAdaptersAddresses; wine-unix 0019 filled each adapter
with a fresh getifaddrs and a socket/two ioctls, including when the IP
interface provider asks for individual NDIS rows.

Refresh one getifaddrs snapshot under if_list_lock at the start of the
read, shared by enumeration, keyed reads and name/index/LUID conversion.
AF_LINK carries flags, index, name, type, physical address, MTU and
counters: derive topology from it too, dropping if_nameindex on iOS and
all per-row socket/ioctl/getifaddrs calls. Keep row fillers on that same
list even across a TTL boundary. Refresh before successful keyed
lookups as well as misses, so removed/replaced identities expire too.

Bound staleness to one second between reads. Failed and successful empty
snapshots expire on the same schedule, without replaying stale data or
retrying per row. Cache each family's route dump (including empty,
denied and failed results) for that period under its own lock; keep the
existing decoder, retry bound and interface identity checks.

Class: ios-port
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-28-nsi-snapshot.md
Offered-upstream: no; iOS one-process runtime integration
